### C++ Implementation (for reference)

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>

double findMedianSortedArrays(const std::vector<int>& A,
                              const std::vector<int>& B) {
    int m = A.size(), n = B.size();
    int total = m + n;
    int mid1 = (total - 1) / 2;
    int mid2 = total / 2;

    int i = 0, j = 0, cnt = 0;
    int median1 = 0, median2 = 0;

    while (cnt <= mid2) {
        int val;
        if (i < m && (j >= n || A[i] <= B[j])) {
            val = A[i++];
        } else {
            val = B[j++];
        }

        if (cnt == mid1) median1 = val;
        if (cnt == mid2) median2 = val;
        ++cnt;
    }

    if (total % 2 == 1) return median2;
    return (median1 + median2) / 2.0;
}
```

Both implementations satisfy the required `O(1)` auxiliary space and `O(n)` time constraints.

**Solution Idea**

The only “extra” data we are allowed to use is the input arrays themselves.  
We can therefore

1. **Sort** both arrays – this rearranges the data in place and costs no
   additional memory.
2. Walk through the two sorted arrays with two indices (`i` and `j`).
   * If `a[i] == b[j]` we have found a common element – print it and
     advance both indices.
   * If `a[i] < b[j]` the current element of `a` is too small – advance `i`.
   * Otherwise advance `j`.

This is the classic “two‑pointer” technique and runs in  
`O(n log n + m log m)` time (dominated by the sorts) and `O(1)` extra space.

